meta {
  name: Update Primary Contact (Duplicate)
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/contacts/{{primaryContactId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

script:pre-request {
  /*
   * Rename the contact **Update Primary Contact** just edited into an identity
   * that already belongs to somebody else — the demo seed's own
   * `demo:contact:35`, whose surname that request derives from. Same full name,
   * same date of birth, same email: the same person by the owner's rule.
   *
   * Built from the surname the previous request set, so this stays a genuine
   * collision on a re-run rather than becoming a no-op.
   */
  bru.setVar(
    "duplicateOfSurname",
    String(bru.getVar("editedContactSurname")),
  );
}

body:json {
  {
    "firstName": "Anurodh",
    "lastName": "{{duplicateOfSurname}}",
    "dateOfBirth": "1978-04-12",
    "email": "anurodh.vaidya@example.com"
  }
}

tests {
  test("the same edit twice is not a conflict — it is the same person", function () {
    // The contact being edited is excluded from its own duplicate check, so
    // re-sending an identical body is a 200, not a 409. Without that exclusion
    // every second save of an unchanged form would fail.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /contacts/:id — the duplicate case (PAC-91 §9)

  This request re-sends the body **Update Primary Contact** just applied, which
  makes it the one case that must *not* be a conflict: a contact is excluded
  from its own identity check, so saving an unchanged form twice is a 200.

  ## The rule

  > A contact is unique on **date of birth + full name + phone or email**. Same
  > name, same DOB and either the same phone *or* the same email is the same
  > person. (David, 2026-09-07.)

  Enforced by `ContactIdentityService`, shared verbatim by lead intake, the
  Household "+ Member" dialog and this endpoint, and backed by two partial
  unique indexes on `contacts` so a race between two writers cannot slip a
  second copy through.

  ## What a real collision returns

  A `PATCH` that would make this contact match a **different** one returns
  `409` with the existing contact's id, so the UI can offer "use the existing
  contact" rather than only refusing:

  ```json
  {
    "statusCode": 409,
    "error": "Conflict",
    "message": "Anurodh Vaidya already exists in this agency",
    "contactId": "665f...b02",
    "householdIds": ["665f...c33"]
  }
  ```

  `householdIds` is a **list** (PAC-91 §5): membership is many-to-many, so the
  single `householdId` this replaces could only ever name one of the households
  the existing contact belongs to. Empty when they belong to none — 322 contacts
  on the production data are in that state.

  ## Why the rule is two indexes, not one

  It is an **OR** — the phone leg *or* the email leg — and every leg is
  optional. On the 2026-09-04 production export 322 contacts have no date of
  birth and 611 have neither phone nor email, so each index is partial on all
  four legs being present and a row missing one is simply not indexed. That is
  the honest position: without a birth date nothing can prove two namesakes are
  the same person, and those cases stay with the fuzzy intake matcher, which
  resolves ambiguity toward "create a new contact" for the same reason —
  a duplicate is recoverable, a merge is not.
}
